157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 50 0 R
/E (As I began this course with Class 9 students, I encountered a wide range of digital exposure among them some were highly active across multiple platforms, while others had limited or supervised access, which created uneven starting points for our group discussions. I noticed that many students displayed overconfidence in their tech knowledge, assuming they were already "safe" online, which led to some initial resistance toward the safety advice I shared. At the same time, I found that students were reluctant to openly share personal experiences of cyberbullying or uncomfortable online interactions, likely due to embarrassment or fear of judgment, so I used anonymous formats to encourage more honest responses. I also observed that peer influence played a strong role at this age, often shaping how seriously students took certain topics based on group reactions rather than individual understanding. I noticed that awareness of real-world consequences varied widely some students clearly connected their online actions to long-term impact, while others did not. I found that engagement was higher during interactive, visual, or activity-based sessions compared to lecture-style explanations, with attention often dipping during theory-heavy discussions. I also remained mindful that sensitive topics such as cyberbullying, body image, or screen addiction resonated personally with some students, so I stayed observant and avoided singling anyone out )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
